    "result_text": "The suite is green. Here's what I implemented.\n\n## Change\n\nThe spec required validating the E.164 national-number length against the country calling code. In `OwnerRestControllerV1`, `normalizeTelephone` previously only enforced the generic E.164 shape (`+` followed by 8\u201315 digits), which is too loose \u2014 e.g. `+61 123` happened to fail only because it was too short overall, and a `+61` number with 8 or 10 national digits would have slipped through.\n\nI added:\n\n1. **`NATIONAL_NUMBER_LENGTHS`** \u2014 a map of country code \u2192 required national-digit count (`\"61\" \u2192 9`, `\"1\" \u2192 10`).\n2. **`validateNationalNumberLength(...)`** \u2014 after the existing shape check, it strips the country code from the significant number and rejects with `InvalidTelephoneException` when the remaining national digits don't match the required count. Unknown country codes are left unchecked.\n\n`InvalidTelephoneException` already maps to **400 Bad Request** in `ExceptionControllerAdvice`, so no advice changes were needed. Both the create and update paths call `normalizeTelephone`, so both are covered. The `\"61\"` vs `\"1\"` prefixes are unambiguous (different first digit), so map iteration order doesn't matter.\n\n## Test result\n\n`AcceptanceTest.errorRejectsWrongNationalLength` (`+61 123` \u2192 400) passes; `BUILD SUCCESS`, `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0`. The `04XXXXXXXX` fixtures in `AcceptanceBase.uniqueTelephone()` normalize to `+61` + 9 national digits, so valid-owner creation still succeeds.",
